import ee 
ee.Authenticate() 
ee.Initialize(project='bright-antonym-495316-g5') 
import geemap.core as geemap 
image = ee.Image('LANDSAT/LC08/C02/T1_TOA/LC08_044034_20140318')
#MULTIPLICATION
mult_sharp=image.select(['B4', 'B3', 'B2']).multiply(image.select([ 'B8']))
#HSV
hsv=image.select(['B4', 'B3', 'B2']).rgbToHsv()
hsv_sharp=ee.Image.cat([
hsv.select('hue'),
hsv.select('saturation'),
image.select('B8')
]).hsvToRgb()
#Simple Brovey method
rgb=image.select(['B4', 'B3', 'B2'])
rgb_sum=image.select('B4').add(image.select('B3')).add(image.select('B2'))
rgb_sum=rgb_sum.where(rgb_sum.eq(0),0.0001)
brovey_sharp=ee.Image.cat([
image.select('B4').divide (rgb_sum).multiply(image.select('B8')), image.select('B3').divide (rgb_sum).multiply (image.select('B8')), image.select('B2').divide (rgb_sum).multiply (image.select('B8')) ]).rename(['B4', 'B3', 'B2'])
#display
Map=geemap.Map(center=[37.7664, -122.44829], zoom=13)
Map.add_layer (image, {'bands': ['B4', 'B3', 'B2'], 'min': 0, 'max': 0.25}, 'Original RGB')
Map.add_layer (mult_sharp, {'bands': ['B4', 'B3', 'B2'], 'min': 0, 'max' :0.05}, 'Multiply')
Map.add_layer (hsv_sharp, {'min': 0, 'max':0.25}, 'HSV')
Map.add_layer (brovey_sharp, {'bands': ['B4', 'B3', 'B2'], 'min': 0, 'max' :0.1}, 'Brovey')
Map



EXPERIMENT NO. 6 
 
●	Title: Implement pan-sharpening algorithm on satellite images 
●	Procedure:  
●	(a)  Perform pansharpening in QGIS. [Download Landsat 8 data (level 2) for any location or consider earlier downloaded data. Create composite of B4,B3,B2 using build virtual raster ->  select Pansharpening option under GDAL ->Raster Miscellaneous  -> add spectral composite and panchromatic (band8) ->Run]  
Steps (USGS EarthExplorer): 
●	Go to EarthExplorer 
●	Select location (map or coordinates) 
●	Go to Data Sets → Landsat → Landsat 8-9 OLI/TIRS Level-1 
●	ONLY DOWNLOAD THESE FILES 
●	From your list, you need ONLY these 4 bands: ● 	..._SR_B2.TIF → Blue 
●	..._SR_B3.TIF → Green 
●	..._SR_B4.TIF → Red 
●	..._B8.TIF → Panchromatic (IMPORTANT) 
●	Click Results 
●	Download the scene (Level-1) 
PART 1: Open QGIS 
1.	Launch QGIS 
2.	You’ll see: 
○ 	Left: Layers panel 
○ 	Center: Map canvas 
PART 2: Load Bands into QGIS 
Method: 
1.	Go to Layer → Add Layer → Add Raster Layer 
2.	Select: 
○ 	B2 
○ 	B3 
○ 	B4 
○ 	B8 
3. 	Click Add 
All bands appear as grayscale images 
PART 3: Create Virtual Raster Now follow your assignment exactly: 
Steps: 
1.	Go to: 
 Raster → Miscellaneous → Build Virtual Raster (VRT) 
2.	In the window: 
○ 	Input layers → Select: 
■ 	B4 
■ 	B3 
■ 	B2 
3. 	Check: 
○ 	✔ Place each input file into a separate band 
4. 	Click Run 
 Output = RGB composite raster 
PART 4: Perform Pansharpening 
Now the main step: 
Steps: 
●	Go to: 
 Raster → Miscellaneous → Pansharpening 
●	In dialog box: 
○ 	Spectral bands → Select your VRT (RGB composite) 
■ 	Steps: 
○ 	Look at the Layers panel (left side) ○ 	You’ll see something like: 
■ 	Virtual Raster ■ 	or Output VRT 
○ 	Click that layer once (to select it) 
○ 	Now open: 
 Processing → Toolbox → Pansharpening 
○ 	In Spectral dataset field: 
■ 	Click the dropdown (small arrow) 
■ 	Select the VRT layer name 
○ 	✔ Done 
○ 	Panchromatic band → Select Band 8 
● 	Choose method:(if there is no this option than run as it is) 
○ 	Try: 
■ 	Brovey (simple) 
■ 	or IHS (better quality) ● 	Output file: 
○ 	Give name like: 
 pansharpened.tif 
● 	Click Run 
PART 5: View Result 
You’ll see: 
● 	Sharper image than original RGB 
● Better spatial resolution PART 6: Compare Before vs After 
You can: 
●	Toggle layers ON/OFF 
●	Zoom in 
You’ll clearly see: 
●	Original → blurry 
●	Pansharpened → sharper 
PART 7: Save Final Output 
1.	Right-click pansharpened layer 
2.	Click: 
 Export → Save As 
3.	Choose: 
○ 	Format: GeoTIFF 
○ 	Location: Your folder 

